  "articleBody": "It's a common feeling when designing a tiny home – you want to maximize space, but sometimes the proposed design feels too rigid or doesn't quite flow with how you imagine living. Don't worry, there are always ways to introduce more flexibility and openness into your custom floor plan! First, reconsider any fixed elements. Could a wall be replaced with a sliding barn door or a curtain? Can built-in seating also serve as storage or a guest bed? Every fixed element should earn its place. Second, look at sightlines. An open sightline from one end of the home to the other can dramatically increase the perceived space. Try to minimize visual barriers and integrate elements that draw the eye through the whole home. Third, incorporate multi-functional zones rather than rigidly defined rooms. For example, your dining area might also be your workspace, or your living room could transform into a sleeping nook. This flexibility is key to feeling less restricted. We often find that clients benefit from exploring variations of their initial ideas. Sometimes a small tweak, like shifting a door or rethinking cabinet depth, can make a huge difference in flow and usability. Remember, a truly custom design should adapt to you, not the other way around. If you're feeling stuck, talking to your designer about these specific concerns can help unlock creative solutions. Zen Tiny Homes specializes in custom floor plans designed around *your* daily life, ensuring it feels expansive and personal.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-02"
